ut after H2 move, the board would have @ at H2. Let's see if opponent can play H1.\n\nFor H1 to be a valid move for opponent, it needs to sandwich @ pieces.\n\nAssuming it's opponent's turn after us.\n\nBut we need to think about what move would least benefit the opponent.\n\nAlternatively, G4 might be better because it strengthens the lower central area.\n\nAnother consideration: mobility. A good move should not only flip pieces but also maximize future options.\n\nLet's analyze G4:\n\nAfter placing @ at G4, flipping F4 and F5.\n\nSo F4 and F5 become @.\n\nCurrent E4, E5, E6 are @, so now F4, F5 are @, and G4 is @.\n\nThis creates a solid block from E4 to G4 vertically? Not exactly, but it's a strong central presence.\n\nAlso, this might open up more moves in the lower part of the board.\n\nH2: flipping G3 and F4.\n\nG3 becomes @, F4 becomes @.\n\nNow, E4 is @, F4 is @, so column 4 has E4, F4, G4 (if G4 is played, but in H2 move, G4 is not played).\n\nWait, in H2 move, F4 is flipped, so F4 becomes @.\n\nSo column 4 would have C4 (*), D4 (*), E4 (@), F4 (@), G4 (empty?), H4 (empty).\n\nWait, after H2 move, F4 is @.\n\nSo column 4: D4 is still * (wait, D4 was * before; in H2 move, F4 is flipped, but D4 remains *).\n\nWait, no: H2 move flips G3 and F4. So F4 changes from * to @.\n\nSo column 4:\n\nA4: empty\n\nB4: empty\n\nC4: *\n\nD4: *\n\nE4: @\n\nF4: @ (after move)\n\nG4: empty\n\nH4: empty\n\nSo column 4 has two *s and two @s.\n\nBut the key is whether this move leads to more control.\n\nAnother point: H2 is on the edge (row H), which is good because edge positions are less likely to be flanked.\n\nSimilarly, B3 and B4 are on row B (second row), which is also an edge row.\n\nIn Reversi, second row positions can be risky because they might lead to opponent taking the corner.\n\nFor example, if we play B4, opponent might respond by taking A4 or A5, potentially leading to corner A8 or A1.\n\nWait, but current board has top rows empty.\n\nLet's consider B4 move.\n\nAf